<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris III of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1894–1943</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tsar Boris III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris III was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1918, when his father Ferdinand abdicated after defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. From 1935 he ruled through a personal regime. He brought Bulgaria into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> in 1941 to regain Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, but refused to declare war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> or deport Bulgaria's own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He died suddenly in 1943.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-iii-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Boris III of Bulgaria" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Karastoyanov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BASA-3K-7-342-28-Boris_III_of_Bulgaria.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
